WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.462
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:10.345
Raise your hand if you think you've
checked your phone more than a dozen times

00:00:10.345 --> 00:00:11.546
already this morning?

00:00:11.546 --> 00:00:14.473
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:00:14.473 --> 00:00:17.489
&gt;&gt; How about if you think you spend too
much time checking email or

00:00:17.489 --> 00:00:19.580
otherwise glued to a piece of technology.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.240
I certainly do.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.400
Too often, I find myself hunched over my
laptop in Bass Library

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.450
trying to make a dent in the day's deluge
of emails.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.200
I'm frantically slamming away at the
keyboard,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.020
looking up at the clock in frustration, as
my free hour of time slips away.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.098
As soon as I think I'm making some
progress, I hit refresh.

00:00:41.098 --> 00:00:42.911
And then.

00:00:42.911 --> 00:00:46.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm right back where I started.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.344
Technology speeds up our lives, but it
comes at a cost.

00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:53.856
We spend so much time reacting,

00:00:53.856 --> 00:00:56.662
getting pulled in a million different
directions at once.

00:00:56.662 --> 00:01:00.580
It makes it really hard to think
deliberately, to live intentionally.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:05.751
Makes it hard to manage stress, foster
creativity.

00:01:05.751 --> 00:01:09.164
But I have good news.

00:01:09.164 --> 00:01:10.430
There's a solution.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.640
It's the oldest trick in the book.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.045
Step outside and get in touch with nature.

00:01:18.045 --> 00:01:20.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:20.974
&gt;&gt; You just might like it.

00:01:20.974 --> 00:01:24.170
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.699
It was a brisk early winter morning in my
hometown of Boulder, Colorado.

00:01:27.699 --> 00:01:32.199
I was just back for the Thanksgiving break
from my first eight weeks of business

00:01:32.199 --> 00:01:34.190
school and man, was I exhausted.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.790
I'd been sprinting non stop to keep up
with everything that this new

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.129
high speed life I'd been throwing at.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.775
Participate in strategy class, so it looks
like I belong in this place.

00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:46.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Drive to a clean tech conference and

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.330
impress those potential employers.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.745
Go out to bars, so I seem fun and make
some friends.

00:01:50.745 --> 00:01:52.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Squeeze every potential

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.170
ounce of value out of this opportunity,
but put on a smile.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.074
And act like it's easy.

00:01:58.074 --> 00:02:00.698
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:05.150
&gt;&gt; I was stressed out, sleep deprived.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:05.980
I didn't feel like myself.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.230
I got out of my car at Boulder Chautauqua
Park Trailhead.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.836
Put my phone on the seat and took off
running up the mud and snow covered trail.

00:02:15.836 --> 00:02:18.112
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:02:18.112 --> 00:02:21.350
I didn't know where I was going, or what I
would do when I got there.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.616
I just knew I needed some space.

00:02:24.616 --> 00:02:26.729
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:02:26.729 --> 00:02:31.590
I found that space on a rock, with a
viewpoint, overlooking the Flat Irons.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.127
As I sat there, a nourishing silence.

00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:38.354
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:02:38.354 --> 00:02:41.305
Began to wash over me.

00:02:41.305 --> 00:02:45.042
The crisp, clear mountain air filled my
lungs.

00:02:45.042 --> 00:02:47.596
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:02:47.596 --> 00:02:52.885
A fresh breeze made the spruce trees sway
peacefully in the wind and

00:02:52.885 --> 00:02:58.183
put a little extra lift under that hawk's
wing soaring overhead.

00:02:58.183 --> 00:03:01.157
I could feel my mind slowing down.

00:03:01.157 --> 00:03:05.173
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:03:05.173 --> 00:03:06.145
About an hour later,

00:03:06.145 --> 00:03:09.399
scribbled some thoughts down in my journal
before returning to my car.

00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:11.001
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:03:11.001 --> 00:03:13.067
Be patient.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.624
Be grateful.

00:03:14.624 --> 00:03:17.455
And get outside.

00:03:17.455 --> 00:03:19.490
I came back to Stanford that next week,
and

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.427
I could feel my feet on the ground again.

00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:25.239
I would look my friends in the eye and be
fully present for my conversations.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:32.090
I began to meditate, a practice that I
have continued to this day.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.574
Nature can change your life.

00:03:35.574 --> 00:03:37.210
And it is right outside your doorstep.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.070
The benefits of interaction with nature
have

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.430
been proven to us in numerous academic
studies.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.120
They include everything from increased
pain tolerance to better memory,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.410
even reduced symptoms of ADHD.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.380
Studies from top universities around the
country and the world show

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.120
that time in nature significantly reduces
stress and increases creativity.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.230
But this wisdom's been around for long
before the academic studies.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.310
Many world languages have words
specifically for

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.450
the concept that getting outside is good
for the mind, body, and spirit.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:24.115
In Norwegian, that word is [FOREIGN].

00:04:24.115 --> 00:04:25.740
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:32.705
&gt;&gt; The Japanese word [FOREIGN] translates
directly into English as forest bathing.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:35.328
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:04:35.328 --> 00:04:36.879
&gt;&gt; Many of our world's most creative and

00:04:36.879 --> 00:04:38.870
brilliant people seem to share the same
wisdom.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.330
Albert Einstein once told us, look deep
into nature and

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:45.860
then you'll understand everything better.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.080
Perhaps, it's no coincidence that both the
Buddha and

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:56.112
Isaac Newton came to their world-altering
realizations while sitting beneath a tree.

00:04:56.112 --> 00:04:58.787
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:04:58.787 --> 00:04:59.600
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.376
&gt;&gt; I wonder what our world might look like
if we all looked deep into nature

00:05:03.376 --> 00:05:04.090
more often.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.687
It shouldn't be that hard, right?

00:05:06.687 --> 00:05:09.219
Can find nature basically everywhere.

00:05:09.219 --> 00:05:15.460
Well, the sad part is, based on the latest
statistics, you probably won't.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.090
As our culture spends more and more time
interacting with technology,

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.990
we're spending less and less time
interacting with nature.

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:26.582
Since the mid-1980s,

00:05:26.582 --> 00:05:29.666
the average time spent outside by the
American child has dropped in half.

00:05:29.666 --> 00:05:31.710
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.920
25% fewer American adults participate in
outdoor recreation.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.779
Meanwhile, the time we spend in front of
screens has skyrocketed.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.595
In the US, we're now up to an average of
seven and a half hours a day.

00:05:45.595 --> 00:05:48.916
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:48.916 --> 00:05:52.570
New York Times bestselling author, Richard
Louv, has a name for this crisis.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:55.690
He calls it Nature Deficit Disorder.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:58.729
And I think it's pretty serious.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:05.270
But the good news is the antidote is
abundant.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.420
In fact, you can find it anywhere on this
campus.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:11.580
You just have to go looking for it.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.930
Louv calls it vitamin N for nature.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:19.750
Take it regularly.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.350
Turn your phone on silent and go lay out
on the grass for a while.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:30.980
Take a walk around and really observe the
intricacies of the bushes and the trees.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:33.920
Open yourself up.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:36.435
Let nature come pouring in.

00:06:36.435 --> 00:06:39.500
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.883
When I finished my hike above Boulder last
Thanksgiving,

00:06:42.883 --> 00:06:45.583
my vitamin N levels were at a pretty good
high.

00:06:45.583 --> 00:06:51.203
But they slowly started coming back down
again over time.

00:06:51.203 --> 00:06:55.220
By the time last summer rolled around, I
was feeling pretty down.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:00.212
This time it was a deeper more existential
kind of low.

00:07:00.212 --> 00:07:04.279
You see my dream job, a summer internship,
turned out to not have this elusive get me

00:07:04.279 --> 00:07:07.100
out of bed in the morning quality I had
been searching for.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.950
I felt lost, confused, alone.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.260
In my search for a happy and meaningful
life.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.910
So at the end of my summer, I decided to
embark on a vision quest.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.842
A two-week introspective journey in
nature,

00:07:27.842 --> 00:07:32.540
culminating with a four-day wilderness
solo in the desert of Southern Utah.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.861
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:07:35.861 --> 00:07:36.511
That journey.

00:07:36.511 --> 00:07:39.321
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:07:39.321 --> 00:07:43.360
The mornings I was woken up with the
desert sunlight on my face.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:48.950
The towering sandstone monoliths that
stood above me and seemed to wait for

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:51.100
me to ask just the right question.

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:55.270
The deafening violence of the
thunderstorm.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:01.933
The deafening silence of the watercolor
sunset.

00:08:01.933 --> 00:08:07.203
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:08:07.203 --> 00:08:09.452
That journey may not fit fully into words,
but

00:08:09.452 --> 00:08:12.289
what I can put into words is the impact
that it's had on me.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:20.858
I now know what it feels like to listen
fully to my own inner compass.

00:08:20.858 --> 00:08:24.061
I've overcome numerous mental and

00:08:24.061 --> 00:08:29.194
emotional barriers that have stifled me my
entire life.

00:08:29.194 --> 00:08:35.630
I found a deeper vision for my future for
how I hope to give my life to this world.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:41.360
That journey in the desert is the reason
that I'm standing here giving this

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:41.958
talk today.

00:08:41.958 --> 00:08:45.464
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:08:45.464 --> 00:08:51.729
So, what's your next step in your own
personal journey in nature?

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:56.400
Doesn't have to be a four-day wilderness
solo,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.140
but I do hope that it pushes you just a
little bit beyond what you're used to.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.050
You might have to invest a little extra
time and effort up front to get out there.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.035
Well, I assure you, the return on your
investment will be worth it.

00:09:12.035 --> 00:09:14.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:18.930
&gt;&gt; You will come back clear headed, more
productive, and less stressed.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.860
And what's more, you will fuel your soul.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.130
For me, nature is the ultimate freedom.

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:34.840
The natural world doesn't judge me.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.530
It welcomes me for exactly who I am.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:43.340
Nature brings me clarity, humility, and
infinite possibility.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:49.460
A starlit sky reminds me that I am so
small, but

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:53.260
that I'm a part of something so big and
incredible.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.600
Nature makes me fully alive and present in
every moment.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.750
A mountaintop view brings me out of my
thoughts and

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:07.730
envelops me in the wonder of the miracle
of life.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:16.040
Thoreau once said, I took a walk in the
woods.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:20.553
And I came out taller than the trees.

00:10:20.553 --> 00:10:25.289
I wonder what some time in nature might
mean for you.

00:10:25.289 --> 00:10:30.026
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:30.026 --> 00:10:34.099
[MUSIC]

